cks, its","   setup, its VM image, and what happens to a job's branch at the end.","-- **Workflows:** series of steps. A step is a prompt, one of the","-  repository's agents or skills, or a shell script. Each step has","-  one text to edit, a dial (a sun when a person decides, a moon when it runs","-  on its own), and the results it passes on.","-- **Capabilities and constraints:** what an agent may call, and the rules","-  its work must keep, each on a ladder from told to enforced.","+- **Workflows:** its series of steps, each step with its settings.","+- **Capabilities and constraints:** what an agent may call, each grant ask","+  or allowed, and the rules its work must keep, each from told to enforced.","+  Both apply to every step of a kind, or to one workflow step."," - **Settings:** its model providers, its people and their roles, its","   notifications, and its secrets.","-- **Records:** its audit log, its evals, its costs, and what landed.","+- **Records:** its jobs, its audit log, its evals, its costs, and what","+  landed."," ","-These are live controls, not a configuration file you edit and restart.","-A server runs on the iii engine, and each value you change in the app","-changes how that engine works for the next call or the next job: which","-model a turn runs on, which calls an agent may make, which image a VM","-boots, what a step does. Bastion's job is to turn the values you set into","-how iii behaves.","+These are live controls, not a configuration file you edit and restart. A","+server runs on the iii engine, and each value you change in the app changes","+how that engine works for the next call or the next job: which model a turn","+runs on, which calls an agent may make, which image a VM boots, what a step","+does. Bastion's job is to turn the values you set into how iii behaves."," ","-The app itself keeps one thing: the list of servers you added. That list","-is the only setting that belongs to you and not to a server.","+The app itself keeps one thing: the list of servers you added. That list is","+the only setting that belongs to you and not to a server."," "," ## Local and remote"," "," A local server starts with the app and needs no credentials. A remote"," server needs a token the server issued you, over TLS. Either kind runs the","-same factory, so a workflow written on one works on the other. A job runs","-on one server; your jobs can run on many.","+same control plane, so a workflow written on one works on the other. A job","+runs on one server; your jobs can run on many."," "," ## What does not change"," "]},{"oldStart":69,"oldLines":9,"newStart":100,"newLines":10,"lines":[" ## Not this"," "," Not an editor: the only code you read in Bastion is the change a job","-proposes. Not a web app. Not a hosted service, for now.","+proposes. Not a web app."," "," ## Next"," ","-Go page by page, on the server level first: each page shows the server it","-belongs to, and its settings move with that server.","+Copy the Short workflow, give its build step its own model and its own","+grants, and send it one request. The job page shows what each step did","+under those settings."]}],"originalFile":"# Bastion's vision\n\n<!-- voice-register: informational -->\n\nBastion is a desktop app a developer uses to run jobs through software\nfactories. A factory is a Bastion server, on your machine or a remote one,\nand you can use several at once. You define what each factory does: its\nworkflows, the capabilities its agents get, and the constraints they work\nunder. Bastion seeds the common workflows with their text filled in, and\nyou can change every step of them. The cost is honest: a factory does only\nwhat its workflows say, so a gate you remove is gone.\n\n## Two levels: each server, and all of them\n\nEvery page shows one server, the one you pick at the top. Some pages can\nalso show every server at once, and those are the ones about records.\n\n**Records, per server or across all.** Jobs, Audit Log, Costs, Evals and\nVerification read one server, or every server you added, side by side. All servers is one more\nchoice in the server picker, so you filter by server the same way on each\nof these pages. You open a job, an entry or a turn and act on it wherever\nit lives.\n\n**Rules, one server at a time.** Workflows, Constraints, Capabilities,\nEngine and Settings belong to the server you pick. A\nrules page never mixes two servers, because each server's rules shape only\nits own jobs, and a sum of two rule sets means nothing.\n\n## What a server holds\n\nA server is a factory, and everything that shapes it lives with it.\n\n- **Workspaces:** the repositories it works in, each with its checks, its\n  setup, its VM image, and what happens to a job's branch at the end.\n- **Workflows:** series of steps. A step is a prompt, one of the\n  repository's agents or skills, or a shell script. Each step has\n  one text to edit, a dial (a sun when a person decides, a moon when it runs\n  on its own), and the results it passes on.\n- **Capabilities and constraints:** what an agent may call, and the rules\n  its work must keep, each on a ladder from told to enforced.\n- **Settings:** its model providers, its people and their roles, its\n  notifications, and its secrets.\n- **Records:** its audit log, its evals, its costs, and what landed.\n\nThese are live controls, not a configuration file you edit and restart.\nA server runs on the iii engine, and each value you change in the app\nchanges how that engine works for the next call or the next job: which\nmodel a turn runs on, which calls an agent may make, which image a VM\nboots, what a step does. Bastion's job is to turn the values you set into\nhow iii behaves.\n\nThe app itself keeps one thing: the list of servers you added. That list\nis the only setting that belongs to you and not to a server.\n\n## Local and remote\n\nA local server starts with the app and needs no credentials. A remote\nserver needs a token the server issued you, over TLS. Either kind runs the\nsame factory, so a workflow written on one works on the other. A job runs\non one server; your jobs can run on many.\n\n## What does not change\n\n- Agent code runs in a VM, never on your machine. A step reaches the\n  network only when its workflow says so, with the secrets it names.\n- Every action names who took it, in the server's audit log.\n- A seeded default is a starting point, never a lock.\n\n## Not this\n\nNot an editor: the only code you read in Bastion is the change a job\nproposes. Not a web app. Not a hosted service, for now.\n\n## Next\n\nGo page by page, on the server level first: each page shows the server it\nbelongs to, and its settings move with that server.\n","userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f574c3ad-b44c-4e4f-b8b5-ec1bf798deb3","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
