X_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; fi' pre-tool-use 3>&1 1>&2; else cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; fi; fi","durationMs":57},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3e6df14e-42c8-4d4e-899a-5bf96a936f9f","timestamp":"2026-11-12T00:58:10.677Z","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"e00d42c2-9006-49f7-ba37-d78d233f29d8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"63076884-86c8-4719-937e-cb437c55717e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QT8wHB58P3ZMuFQsZmwhTr","type":"tool_result","content":"## Decisions\n\nEach decision keeps the number the code cites. Numbers not listed were\nretired with the old plan.\n\n| # | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| D1 | Rust for all Bastion code. Another language only where no Rust path exists, named here with its reason: `.pi/extensions/entire/index.ts` is generated by the Entire tool and is not Bastion code. |\n| D4 | One dark theme, purple as the main color. Its tokens live in `crates/bastion-desktop/src/theme.rs`. |\n| D6 | Every Bastion worker is a Rust binary on `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, started by compose through `scripts.run`. |\n76:| D47 | A link may be a failure link: when its step fails, the job goes along it instead of back to the step that writes. A failure link orders nothing, so it may go back to an earlier step; a step reached only by failure links is skipped otherwise. The failing step's revisions bound any loop, and past them a person decides. A step has at most one failure link. |\n77:| D48 | A grant is ask or allowed; a call no grant covers is refused, so there is no denied grant. Demoting a grant from ask removes it. A step's own grants, when it has any, take the place of its kind's, so a step is narrowed by listing what it may call. This replaces D44's \"wins over its kind's\". |\n| D41 | A job ends with its branch ready. When the last step passes, a workspace set to merge merges the branch into its base on the host; one set to leave keeps the branch. |\n| D42 | Governance is per step. A step may change files or only reads, and the gate refuses a write from a step that only reads. Dials, notices, artifacts and evals name steps. |\n| D43 | A step may name its own model and budget. The model wins over the job's and the server's. The budget is how often its failure may send work back (two by default) and the most its turns may cost; past the cost, Bastion starts no more turns there by itself, but a person's retry, note or answer still runs. |\n| D44 | A step is known as `workflow/step`. A grant may be for one workflow step, and it wins over the grant for its kind; a constraint may apply at some steps only, and with none named it applies at every step. The gate reads the step from the turn. |\n| D45 | A step is a prompt, an agent, a skill or a shell script. A repository's command is not a kind of its own: a prompt that reads its file does what it did, and that is what migration 7 makes of each command step. This narrows D39. |\n| D46 | A repository's charter is `AGENTS.md` and `.agents/`: `.agents/settings.json` holds its permissions and hooks, and `.agents/agents` and `.agents/skills` hold the agents and skills its steps run. Bastion assumes no one vendor's files. The harness, the rules on an agent's calls, is Bastion's own. |\n| D47 | A link may be a failure link: when its step fails, the job goes along it instead of back to the step that writes. A failure link orders nothing, so it may go back to an earlier step; a step reached only by failure links is skipped otherwise. The failing step's revisions bound any loop, and past them a person decides. A step has at most one failure link. |\n| D48 | A grant is ask or allowed; a call no grant covers is refused, so there is no denied grant. Demoting a grant from ask removes it. A step's own grants, when it has any, take the place of its kind's, so a step is narrowed by listing what it may call. This replaces D44's \"wins over its kind's\". |\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Dark to merge.** ghola never lets a factory merge unattended; Bastion's\n  dark dial can run to merge. Should merge always need a person, or a machine\n  verdict, such as the `verify.yaml` contract?\n- **Configuration in git.** ghola keeps configuration in files under review;\n  Bastion keeps it in the database with versions (D26). Should rules also be\n  importable as files, such as team rule packs shared by servers?\n- **Multi-user hosting.** A shared server with people and TLS works today.\n  Is hosted Bastion a goal?\n- **Unused engine workers.** C4 lists them; some may be the harness's\n  dependencies.\n- **Harness and factory.** D25's two layers are collapsing onto workflows\n  and their steps: a step's grants, constraints, model and budget all live\n  on the step. Should the split stay, or should Bastion apply everything\n  from the settings of a workflow and its steps?","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d0847fe8-fbdc-433a-b8ce-9d79ebda93ad","timestamp":"2026-11-12T00:58:11.291Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Decisions\n\nEach decision keeps the number the code cites. Numbers not listed were\nretired with the old plan.\n\n| # | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| D1 | Rust for all Bastion code. Another language only where no Rust path exists, named here with its reason: `.pi/extensions/entire/index.ts` is generated by the Entire tool and is not Bastion code. |\n| D4 | One dark theme, purple as the main color. Its tokens live in `crates/bastion-desktop/src/theme.rs`. |\n| D6 | Every Bastion worker is a Rust binary on `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, started by compose through `scripts.run`. |\n76:| D47 | A link may be a failure link: when its step fails, the job goes along it instead of back to the step that writes. A failure link orders nothing, so it may go back to an earlier step; a step reached only by failure links is skipped otherwise. The failing step's revisions bound any loop, and past them a person decides. A step has at most one failure link. |\n77:| D48 | A grant is ask or allowed; a call no grant covers is refused, so there is no denied grant. Demoting a grant from ask removes it. A step's own grants, when it has any, take the place of its kind's, so a step is narrowed by listing what it may call. This replaces D44's \"wins over its kind's\". |\n| D41 | A job ends with its branch ready. When the last step passes, a workspace set to merge merges the branch into its base on the host; one set to leave keeps the branch. |\n| D42 | Governance is per step. A step may change files or only reads, and the gate refuses a write from a step that only reads. Dials, notices, artifacts and evals name steps. |\n| D43 | A step may name its own model and budget. The model wins over the job's and the server's. The budget is how often its failure may send work back (two by default) and the most its turns may cost; past the cost, Bastion starts no more turns there by itself, but a person's retry, note or answer still runs. |\n| D44 | A step is known as `workflow/step`. A grant may be for one workflow step, and it wins over the grant for its kind; a constraint may apply at some steps only, and with none named it applies at every step. The gate reads the step from the turn. |\n| D45 | A step is a prompt, an agent, a skill or a shell script. A repository's command is not a kind of its own: a prompt that reads its file does what it did, and that is what migration 7 makes of each command step. This narrows D39. |\n| D46 | A repository's charter is `AGENTS.md` and `.agents/`: `.agents/settings.json` holds its permissions and hooks, and `.agents/agents` and `.agents/skills` hold the agents and skills its steps run. Bastion assumes no one vendor's files. The harness, the rules on an agent's calls, is Bastion's own. |\n| D47 | A link may be a failure link: when its step fails, the job goes along it instead of back to the step that writes. A failure link orders nothing, so it may go back to an earlier step; a step reached only by failure links is skipped otherwise. The failing step's revisions bound any loop, and past them a person decides. A step has at most one failure link. |\n| D48 | A grant is ask or allowed; a call no grant covers is refused, so there is no denied grant. Demoting a grant from ask removes it. A step's own grants, when it has any, take the place of its kind's, so a step is narrowed by listing what it may call. This replaces D44's \"wins over its kind's\". |\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Dark to merge.** ghola never lets a factory merge unattended; Bastion's\n  dark dial can run to merge. Should merge always need a person, or a machine\n  verdict, such as the `verify.yaml` contract?\n- **Configuration in git.** ghola keeps configuration in files under review;\n  Bastion keeps it in the database with versions (D26). Should rules also be\n  importable as files, such as team rule packs shared by servers?\n- **Multi-user hosting.** A shared server with people and TLS works today.\n  Is hosted Bastion a goal?\n- **Unused engine workers.** C4 lists them; some may be the harness's\n  dependencies.\n- **Harness and factory.** D25's two layers are collapsing onto workflows\n  and their steps: a step's grants, constraints, model and budget all live\n  on the step. Should the split stay, or should Bastion apply everything\n  from the settings of a workflow and its steps?","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d392f616-87d1-4246-94af-3db1df0ea53d","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e00d42c2-9006-49f7-ba37-d78d233f29d8","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
